1
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:37,760
♪ I woke up this morning ♪

2
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:43,680
♪ with my mind stayed on freedom ♪

3
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,520
♪ I woke up this morning with my mind, Lord ♪

4
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:52,360
♪ Stayed on freedom. ♪

5
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,800
Hey, you. You don't go that way.

6
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:17,720
That way is not for coloreds.

7
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,880
Get off, go around the back and get on there.

8
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,480
That's your entrance. You know that.

9
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,000
I don't see the need...

10
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:29,320
for getting off, then back on there,

11
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:32,480
not when there is folks standing in the stairwell.

12
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:35,920
How am I gonna squeeze on there?

13
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:38,800
If you ain't going through that back door,

14
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:41,080
you're getting off my bus.

15
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:42,200
Sir, let me go.

16
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,400
Please don't do that, sir. Stop!

17
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:05,200
You better not hit me.

18
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,920
Get out that door!

19
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:26,720
Hey!

20
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:29,160
Hey!

21
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,120
Hey, hold on. Stop!

22
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:10,440
Nearly.

23
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:15,520
- Sheffield? - Almost. Really close.

24
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So, not Sheffield then.

25
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,960
You're doing this deliberately, aren't you?

26
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,200
Who are you talking to?

27
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,720
If it's me, I haven't touched anything.

28
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:23,000
I'm talking to the TARDIS.

29
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:25,280
'Cause this is our ninth attempt.

30
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:28,000
Fourteenth. You can't control this thing, can you?

31
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:29,920
Excuse me? Yes, I can.

32
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:31,120
Most of the time.

33
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:33,520
Just sometimes, like now, it has a mind of its own.

34
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,560
So, where are we, actually?

35
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:38,120
Earth. United States.

36
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:42,200
1955, Montgomery, Alabama,

37
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:43,520
if I'm reading this properly.

38
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,680
New displays. Still figuring them out.

39
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:47,520
1955?

40
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,080
Elvis. Could we see Elvis?

41
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:50,600
I think he's in New York this week.

42
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:51,800
I could give him a call.

43
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:53,320
You haven't got Elvis's phone number?

44
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,600
Don't ever tell anyone I lent him a mobile phone.

45
00:03:58,600 --> 00:03:59,800
Whoa.

46
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:01,560
What's that?

47
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:03,120
Traces of Artron energy?

48
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:04,680
Should we know what Artron energy is?

49
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,960
It's the same type of energy that TARDIS runs off.

50
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,360
There really shouldn't be traces of Artron energy here.

51
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:11,040
Unless they are ours, which they're not.

52
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,400
And now you want to check it out?

53
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:13,480
Yeah, I should.

54
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,000
Quick look, but quietly. History is very delicate.

55
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,320
We stick together.

56
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:26,040
Real life 1950s. Time travel's awesome!

57
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,200
Excuse me?

58
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:30,560
Excuse me?

59
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,400
You dropped this.

60
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:39,680
Get your filthy black hands off my wife.

61
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,120
- Stop it! - Please, take a step back.

62
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:43,520
Are you okay, Ryan?

63
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:45,400
I was just trying to give her back her glove.

64
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:46,560
Is this your boy?

65
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:47,840
It's my grandson, actually.

66
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,800
- Your what? - My grandson.

67
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:51,960
You ain't from around here.

68
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:53,120
We don't want any trouble.

69
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,520
I don't know how it goes where you folks are from,

70
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:58,720
but your boy, he'll be swinging from a tree with a noose for a neckerchief

71
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,960
if he touches a white woman in Montgomery.

72
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:03,040
- What did you just... - Is there a problem here, Mr Steele?

73
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:05,200
Step away now, go ahead. Step away.

74
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:09,040
- Friends of yours? - No, sir.

75
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:12,240
Just on my lunch break, wondering if I can help out

76
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:13,920
with any misunderstanding.

77
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,640
I believe your suit will be ready tomorrow.

78
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,920
The alterations are going to make it look just right, sir.

79
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:27,960
Let's go, Lizzie.

80
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,640
- Are you crazy? - He slapped me.

81
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,960
Do you read the newspapers?

82
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:36,000
You know what they did to young Emmett Till.

83
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:39,080
- We're from out of town. - So was Emmett Till.

84
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,680
On vacation from the north, couple of words to...

85
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,920
a white woman in Mississippi, and the next thing,

86
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:50,640
they find his body in the river.

87
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,360
You want that to be you?

88
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:54,640
No.

89
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,000
Did your mother raise you with no manners?

90
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:58,600
I will take a "No, ma'am."

91
00:05:58,960 --> 00:05:59,960
No, ma'am.

92
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:03,360
- You all together? - Yes, we're very grateful, Miss...

93
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,640
Mrs Parks. Rosa Parks.

94
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:08,240
No way.

95
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:10,080
- You kidding? - Brilliant!

96
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:13,720
Rosa Parks? Lovely to meet you, Rosa Parks. Big fan.

97
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:14,880
Excuse me?

98
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:16,760
Big fan of...

99
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:18,320
Montgomery.

100
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:20,640
I am... We are just visiting.

101
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:25,200
Recommend anything for tourists like us?

102
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:28,560
I recommend you get yourselves the hell out of Alabama

103
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,160
before you find yourselves in trouble you can't get out of.

104
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:36,600
Oh, my God.

105
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,040
Can you believe it? Actual Rosa Parks.

106
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:40,120
Amazing.

107
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:42,600
Also a problem.

108
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:45,240
Registering traces of Artron energy all around her.

109
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:46,840
Why is that?

110
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:01,480
No.

111
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:24,640
Force shields.

112
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:32,240
We were in Rosa Parks class in primary.

113
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:33,440
Do you remember?

114
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:36,920
All the Year 4, 5 and 6 classes were named after inspirational people.

115
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,200
She's the bus woman, right?

116
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:39,800
- You do remember what she did? - Yeah.

117
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,480
First black woman to ever drive a bus.

118
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:43,120
- No, Ryan. - What?

119
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,560
Your nan would have a fit right now.

120
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:48,200
How could you have been in a class named after the woman,

121
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:49,440
and not know who she is?

122
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:50,520
She's American.

123
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:53,800
She refused to give up her seat on a segregated bus to a white passenger.

124
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,800
And got arrested for it.

125
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:58,800
Her arrest started a boycott of the buses in Montgomery.

126
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,200
Or rather, will start.

127
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:03,920
Today is Wednesday, November 30th, 1955.

128
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:07,560
Tomorrow, Rosa refuses to give up her seat.

129
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:10,280
And all this basically kicked off the US civil rights movement

130
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:12,120
led by Martin Luther King.

131
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:13,880
See, I'm not totally ignorant.

132
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,080
I just got confused by the whole bus thing.

133
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:19,400
Martin Luther King is a minister here in Montgomery, right now.

134
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:21,080
- He and Rosa knew each other? - Yeah.

135
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:24,600
Is it me, or has it gone very quiet in here?

136
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:30,160
We don't serve Negroes.

137
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:37,440
Good.

138
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:39,000
Because I don't eat them.

139
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,120
Or Mexicans.

140
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:43,360
Is she talking to me?

141
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:45,640
You all need to eat somewhere else.

142
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:52,520
Come on.

143
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:02,320
Okay, listen, I can deal with this.

144
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,280
You guys go back to the TARDIS and be safe.

145
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:05,760
- While you do what? - Locate the source

146
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:07,480
of these energy anomalies.

147
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:09,760
We're one day out of a tipping point in earth history,

148
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:11,720
I don't want anything disrupting that.

149
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:14,000
It's easy for me here.

150
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:15,320
It's more dangerous for you.

151
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:17,600
You can walk away from this.

152
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:20,360
- Rosa Parks can't. - Rosa Parks doesn't.

153
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:23,920
If she can live here all her life, a couple of hours ain't gonna kill me.

154
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:26,120
- It ain't gonna kill me, right? - No.

155
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:27,760
Not if we look out for each other.

156
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:30,920
I'm cool with it. What do you reckon, Mexican lady?

157
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:34,520
Oi, keep that up, I'll use you as a piñata.

158
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:37,520
Epicentre of the Artron region is 1.2 miles that way.

159
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:38,600
- Come on. - Hey, hey.

160
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:40,680
We will stop somewhere else to eat, though, won't we?

161
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:42,000
No time, Graham.

162
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:43,880
Have you noticed that happens a lot?

163
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:46,560
I need regular food, that's all.

164
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:49,440
We just got thrown out of a bar and that's what you're worried about?

165
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:50,520
Not just that.

166
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:53,360
Not sure your stomach's compatible with time travel, Graham.

167
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,280
Can I help you?

168
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:16,800
There a problem here?

169
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,080
No problem, ma'am.

170
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:22,480
No problem at all.

171
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:31,240
This is where the Artron signals converge.

172
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:34,200
At the bus company. All roads lead to Rosa Parks.

173
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:35,280
Yeah.

174
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:37,880
Bit of a worry. Let's have a look around.

175
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:39,640
I'm not sure about this.

176
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:45,640
There's nothing in here.

177
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:47,280
Why padlock an empty room?

178
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:48,560
Unless it's not empty.

179
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,240
That wasn't there a second ago.

180
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:55,440
No kidding.

181
00:10:55,520 --> 00:10:57,400
It was there. We just couldn't see it.

182
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:58,920
Perception filter.

183
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:01,720
Why would anybody do that to a suitcase?

184
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:05,680
Can we open it?

185
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:07,640
Is the right question.

186
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:15,200
Is anyone excited? 'Cause I'm really excited.

187
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:16,720
You won't be if it's a bomb.

188
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:18,920
Don't kill the vibe, Graham.

189
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:22,920
Whoa. Not very '50s.

190
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,200
I knew it.

191
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:26,240
See, now there is a problem.

192
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,520
We are not the only ones in Montgomery who don't belong here.

193
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:30,160
Any clues what any of that is?

194
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:32,360
Information brick.

195
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:35,600
Multi-intercepting surveillance device.

196
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:36,800
All a bit knackered though.

197
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:38,360
Well, why has it been left in here?

198
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:43,080
This explains the Artron signals.

199
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:45,280
This is very bad news.

200
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:46,960
Secondary charger for a...

201
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:54,200
In here.

202
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:12,720
Stay here.

203
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:20,920
Oi, Brando. Looking for us?

204
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,360
I'm not armed.

205
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:26,120
Is that suppose to make me not shoot you?

206
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:27,280
Ideally.

207
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:29,080
So,

208
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:31,680
temporal displacement weapon.

209
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:33,320
Horrible things. Can't stand them.

210
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:34,400
Thank you.

211
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:35,680
Not a compliment.

212
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:39,280
Takes a lot of power to displace things in time.

213
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:42,680
I think you and I both know your weapon is pretty much out of juice.

214
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:45,120
And I've got your spare battery.

215
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:49,800
You've been leaving traces of residual Artron energy all over 1955.

216
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,440
What are you, the Artron police?

217
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:56,440
Maybe you are. Blue box in the alley, is it a TARDIS?

218
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,640
Might be. What's it to you?

219
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,120
It could be worth a lot.

220
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:02,000
Nah, not that one.

221
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:05,320
Second hand, huge mileage. One careless owner.

222
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:08,440
Mind you, it's better than a Vortex Manipulator,

223
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:10,080
like the one on your wrist.

224
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,160
Cheap and nasty time travel.

225
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:16,840
So, what do you want with Rosa Parks?

226
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:18,920
Who?

227
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:21,080
Now you're being annoying.

228
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:22,440
The feeling's mutual.

229
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:23,560
How long have you been here?

230
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:24,720
Get out of Montgomery.

231
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:26,800
You're not the first to say that to us.

232
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:30,360
If I see any of you again, I will kill you.

233
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:32,800
Don't threaten me.

234
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:41,960
Chop chop, on your way now.

235
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:48,240
What are you doing?

236
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:49,680
Come on, gang.

237
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:53,280
Are we actually leaving?

238
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:54,920
Not in a million years.

239
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:10,440
I'm getting pretty sick of seeing that sign.

240
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:12,880
So, how are we gonna do this?

241
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:15,680
In you come before anyone sees.

242
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:17,360
Well, this is fun.

243
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:21,120
Last time I sneaked into someone's room by a window was Danny Biswas in Year 10.

244
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:23,720
You just went right down in my estimation.

245
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:25,480
All right!

246
00:14:25,560 --> 00:14:28,040
Danny Biswas? He was punching well above his weight.

247
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:31,200
Did you just accidentally pay me a compliment?

248
00:14:32,920 --> 00:14:33,920
Whatever.

249
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:36,400
Why can't we just stay in the TARDIS?

250
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:39,360
Our friend with the temporal displacement weapon has got eyes on it.

251
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:41,680
We go in and out of there, we're gonna run into him again.

252
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:42,880
And I don't want to, just yet.

253
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:44,840
- Not until we figure this out. - This is better?

254
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:46,800
Smuggling ourselves into crummy motels?

255
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:48,800
I just need a moment to work this out.

256
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:50,880
Yeah, like, what do we actually know?

257
00:14:50,960 --> 00:14:54,000
Well, 1955, Montgomery.

258
00:14:54,080 --> 00:14:57,200
One day away from Rosa Parks refusing to give her seat up on a bus.

259
00:14:57,280 --> 00:14:59,440
Meanwhile, we've got an out of time impostor,

260
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:02,200
skulking in the bus works with a temporal displacement weapon.

261
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:06,200
I understand "weapon," but not sure about "temporal displacement."

262
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:08,120
Displaces you through time.

263
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:10,400
A blast hits you, you're thrown through time

264
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:12,040
to wherever the user has set it.

265
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:13,840
He had it set for the very far future.

266
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:14,960
That's horrible.

267
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:17,400
Yes, and it uses Artron molecules.

268
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:20,400
That, plus his Vortex Manipulator

269
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:23,640
were presumably what the TARDIS detected when it brought us here.

270
00:15:23,720 --> 00:15:25,680
So, do we think he is trying to kill Rosa Parks?

271
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:26,800
Or use that weapon on her?

272
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:29,600
He is been here a while. Set up base at the bus works.

273
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:32,160
If he wanted to get rid of her, why has he not done it already?

274
00:15:32,520 --> 00:15:34,640
Besides, the sonic picked up something else on him,

275
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:36,240
but I need to go back to make sure.

276
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:37,320
Right.

277
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:40,120
What do you remember about Rosa from school?

278
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:41,760
Oi, whoa. What are you doing?

279
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,640
That is vandalism. We'll have to pay for that!

280
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:45,160
Don't worry. Special pen.

281
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:47,760
No, pack it in. You ain't Banksy.

282
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:49,560
Or am I?

283
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:54,240
Rosa takes the bus on the first of December, 1955.

284
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,640
What time of day?

285
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:57,800
Evening. She was coming home from work.

286
00:15:57,880 --> 00:15:59,960
I remember because she said, people thought she didn't stand

287
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,720
because she was tired from working, but she wasn't.

288
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:02,760
Where did she work?

289
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,160
I think it was in a shop, like a department store.

290
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:08,320
No, no, wait, earlier she told that git that slapped Ryan,

291
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:09,880
"Your suit will be ready tomorrow."

292
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,800
That's it, she did clothes repairs. She was a seamstress.

293
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:15,040
I mean, is a seamstress.

294
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:16,640
Can't get used to being in the past.

295
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:21,440
- Bathroom. - Seriously?

296
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:22,920
Very seriously.

297
00:16:26,080 --> 00:16:28,120
No, Doc, the wall.

298
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:33,520
Banksy doesn't have one of those.

299
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:35,800
Or have I?

300
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:39,760
Oh, Officer. What can I do for you?

301
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,440
Can I come in, ma'am?

302
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,920
"Ma'am," I still can't get used to that.

303
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:47,520
Here we are, darling.

304
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,200
This police officer was so keen to come in

305
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:50,760
he didn't feel the need to introduce himself.

306
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:52,600
Is there a problem, Officer...

307
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:55,160
Mason. Montgomery police.

308
00:16:56,920 --> 00:16:59,280
I'd offer you a cup but the refreshment facilities are very poor.

309
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:00,560
I'll be leaving a note.

310
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:01,920
British?

311
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:03,480
How can you tell?

312
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:05,640
You've been making folks uneasy.

313
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:06,920
How have we done that?

314
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:10,640
You don't happen to know a couple of mongrels, hmm?

315
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:13,680
Negro boy. Mexican girl.

316
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:16,160
I don't recognise anyone by that description.

317
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:20,480
They say that Negro has been going around

318
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:24,080
picking fights with upstanding citizens.

319
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:28,080
You appreciate it's a... It's an offence

320
00:17:28,160 --> 00:17:30,400
to harbour coloreds in a room here.

321
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:33,120
We're not harbouring anyone who doesn't have the right to be here.

322
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:34,160
Mmm-hmm.

323
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:39,240
What's your business here in Montgomery?

324
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:42,080
We are here to pitch an invention.

325
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:46,240
Yeah, uh, it's a telephone.

326
00:17:47,720 --> 00:17:53,720
That plays music and it's a camera, also takes photos.

327
00:17:53,960 --> 00:17:55,640
And it's a calendar.

328
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:58,000
And it sends letters.

329
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:00,640
- Sounds ridiculous. - Yeah...

330
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:02,800
What's your name, sir?

331
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:04,560
Steve.

332
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:08,920
Jobs. Steve Jobs.

333
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,560
You being disrespectful with me, MrJobs?

334
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:14,720
Steve Jobs would never disrespect a Montgomery police officer, sir.

335
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:37,520
You get yourselves gone as soon as your business is concluded.

336
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:39,200
Mmm-hmm.

337
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:46,400
I did not warm to him.

338
00:18:48,680 --> 00:18:50,920
I'm sick of this place already.

339
00:18:51,080 --> 00:18:52,560
I know.

340
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:55,880
To be here just as history is taking place.

341
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:57,840
This ain't history here, Yaz.

342
00:18:57,960 --> 00:18:59,360
We're hiding behind bins.

343
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:04,320
I'm having to work so hard to keep my temper, every second here.

344
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:07,560
I could have slapped that guy back there as soon as we arrived.

345
00:19:08,520 --> 00:19:10,840
Thank God, my nan taught me how to keep my temper.

346
00:19:11,720 --> 00:19:13,800
Never give them the excuse.

347
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:15,160
Yep.

348
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:17,600
- My dad tells me the same. - Yeah, see.

349
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:22,320
It's not like Rosa Parks wipes out racism from the world forever.

350
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:26,520
Otherwise, how come I get stopped way more by the police than my white mates?

351
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:29,280
Oi, not this police.

352
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:30,880
Tell me you don't get hassle.

353
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:34,160
Of course, I do. Especially on the job.

354
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:36,440
I get called a Paki when I'm sorting out a domestic,

355
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:39,200
or a terrorist on the way home from the mosque.

356
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:40,720
Yeah, exactly.

357
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:43,440
But they don't win, those people.

358
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,320
I can be a police officer now

359
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:47,720
because people like Rosa Parks fought those battles for me.

360
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:49,000
For us.

361
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:52,600
And in 53 years they'll have a black president as leader.

362
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:56,640
Who knows where they'll be 50 years after that?

363
00:19:57,480 --> 00:19:59,560
That's proper change.

364
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:05,960
- What? - Were you born this positive?

365
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:08,360
Guess so.

366
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:10,040
Must be my Mexican blood.

367
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:15,560
Yaz? Ryan?

368
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:23,160
Where were we? We need as much intel as we can get.

369
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:26,120
If we're gonna protect Rosa, we need to know the facts of her life.

370
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:29,160
Home address. Daily routine. Where she works.

371
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:31,880
The route she takes and the church she attends.

372
00:20:31,960 --> 00:20:34,400
Also the name of the driver she refused.

373
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,080
I know that. It's James Blake.

374
00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:37,480
How do you know that?

375
00:20:37,560 --> 00:20:41,120
Well, your nan when she found out I was a bus driver, said to me,

376
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:43,640
"You better not be like James Blake.

377
00:20:43,720 --> 00:20:45,600
"Blake the snake." That's what she called him.

378
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:47,640
And I had to ask her who he was,

379
00:20:47,720 --> 00:20:49,560
and she just said he gave all bus drivers a bad name.

380
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:51,280
She said that when you'd only just met?

381
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:52,960
- Yeah. - That's pretty hardline.

382
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:54,440
That's my nan.

383
00:20:54,560 --> 00:20:57,640
Yeah. She had a t-shirt that said,

384
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:00,120
"Spirit of Rosa."

385
00:21:02,680 --> 00:21:04,920
And well, I wish that she was here.

386
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,840
I don't. She'd start a riot.

387
00:21:13,880 --> 00:21:15,560
Right. Operation Rosa Parks.

388
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:35,840
I found out where she works.

389
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:37,960
Montgomery Fair Department store.

390
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:41,440
And if the phone book's right, this is where she lives.

391
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:46,600
Right. So, these bus routes don't go near Rosa's shop or house,

392
00:21:46,680 --> 00:21:49,360
but these ones do.

393
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:52,840
Great. If we can find Rosa's route and time today,

394
00:21:52,920 --> 00:21:54,320
we can keep an eye on her tomorrow.

395
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:56,320
Who's up for a bus ride?

396
00:21:59,760 --> 00:22:01,440
GRAHAM. Sir, excuse me.

397
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:02,760
Sorry.

398
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:04,680
Hey, Doc.

399
00:22:06,120 --> 00:22:10,280
This route is one of three that goes between Rosa's home and where she works.

400
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:12,000
I reckon this one's the most direct.

401
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:14,320
So it's most likely she takes this one tomorrow night.

402
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:16,080
But we can't be certain exactly what time.

403
00:22:16,160 --> 00:22:18,080
Unless we stalk her all day.

404
00:22:21,280 --> 00:22:23,640
This is me. On the back of the bus.

405
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:27,120
I'm so ashamed. You shouldn't have to do this.

406
00:22:27,200 --> 00:22:28,920
I agree. I'm sorry, Ryan.

407
00:22:30,440 --> 00:22:32,400
The driver let me on at the front of the bus.

408
00:22:32,480 --> 00:22:34,840
What does that mean for where I sit?

409
00:22:35,880 --> 00:22:38,920
Obviously not a lot of Pakistani heritage around here.

410
00:22:39,720 --> 00:22:42,600
Does coloured just mean black in 1955?

411
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:46,480
I guess I'll park my South Asian Mexican backside

412
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:48,760
in the white section and let's see what happens.

413
00:22:50,440 --> 00:22:54,160
Riding the bus in Montgomery. Good times.

414
00:23:04,040 --> 00:23:05,880
That's where she works.

415
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:07,600
This must be her stop.

416
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:10,000
Perfect. We can wait here,

417
00:23:10,080 --> 00:23:12,200
then get on the bus with her when she finishes work.

418
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:13,520
Have a little chat.

419
00:23:18,800 --> 00:23:21,360
Hi. Nice to see you again.

420
00:23:21,480 --> 00:23:22,880
Mrs Parks, right?

421
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:24,040
Yes.

422
00:23:24,120 --> 00:23:27,000
You helped us out earlier today with our little misunderstanding.

423
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:29,680
I remember. British.

424
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:32,160
Yes, funny thing. You'll never believe this.

425
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:35,200
We're actually doing market research for bus companies.

426
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:37,080
Looking at people's transport habits.

427
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:40,400
Answer the questions, enter the raffle for a prize.

428
00:23:40,560 --> 00:23:42,880
You get this bus this time every day?

429
00:23:43,040 --> 00:23:45,640
Most days. Always this time.

430
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:47,120
And you live?

431
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:49,360
Cleveland Court. Next stop.

432
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:56,640
Ma'am, if you keep sitting there we're all going to have to move.

433
00:23:57,640 --> 00:23:59,120
What do you mean?

434
00:24:00,040 --> 00:24:03,160
Well, if white folks need seats,

435
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:06,240
by law, I have to give mine up.

436
00:24:06,320 --> 00:24:09,600
This middle section's only for coloreds if white folk don't need it.

437
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:11,200
Well, that's not right.

438
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,240
Tell that to your company, sir.

439
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:17,200
If I win your raffle, will that give me

440
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:21,280
the right to sit anywhere I want on this bus?

441
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:23,520
No.

442
00:24:25,120 --> 00:24:27,360
I didn't think so.

443
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:29,520
This is my stop.

444
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:34,320
I'm gonna follow her.

445
00:24:34,440 --> 00:24:35,760
See what I can find out.

446
00:24:35,880 --> 00:24:37,720
Meet you back at the motel later.

447
00:24:38,440 --> 00:24:40,040
Ryan.

448
00:24:40,120 --> 00:24:41,760
Be careful.

449
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:43,320
Yeah.

450
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:46,280
Do you think he'll be all right?

451
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:47,560
I hope so.

452
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:50,560
Graham, do you think you can find James Blake, the driver?

453
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:53,840
Um, yeah, I'll just have a talk with the bloke at the front

454
00:24:53,880 --> 00:24:55,760
and find out where they all drink.

455
00:24:55,840 --> 00:24:58,560
Yaz, can you compile a timeline of what happens,

456
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:00,120
this time tomorrow evening?

457
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:01,920
Okay. But what are you gonna do?

458
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Talk to our friend. Tell him to stay out of history's way.

459
00:25:17,960 --> 00:25:21,600
Stalking Rosa Parks! Don't know about this.

460
00:25:24,680 --> 00:25:26,680
You better not be following me.

461
00:25:28,280 --> 00:25:30,280
- I wanna help. - With what?

462
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:33,960
The fight.

463
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:40,680
And how do I know you're not a spy for the police or the FBI?

464
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:42,720
Because if they were gonna send a spy

465
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:45,600
they would send one who could actually follow you without being spotted.

466
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:47,320
And that didn't have a British accent.

467
00:25:47,920 --> 00:25:50,640
Also, don't think they know any black guys.

468
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,240
You any good at serving coffee?

469
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:41,480
Oops. Shame.

470
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:43,840
You just sent all your equipment to goodness knows where.

471
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:47,640
79th century judging by the weapon setting

472
00:26:47,720 --> 00:26:50,240
which, by the way, overheat very easily.

473
00:26:51,560 --> 00:26:53,600
Cheap and nasty. Now we're even.

474
00:26:55,640 --> 00:26:57,440
First things first.

475
00:26:59,120 --> 00:27:00,840
Tell me about Stormcage.

476
00:27:01,560 --> 00:27:04,520
- Storm what? - Ah, rubbish liar.

477
00:27:04,600 --> 00:27:07,760
On your wrist is a Stormcage identifier.

478
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:10,800
The most secure prison facility this side of the universe.

479
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:12,280
Not in this time zone, of course.

480
00:27:12,360 --> 00:27:15,040
But I guess that's where the Vortex Manipulator comes in.

481
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:16,600
It's amazing what you can get

482
00:27:16,680 --> 00:27:19,080
if you're prepared to work and barter inside that prison.

483
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:20,640
Escape or release?

484
00:27:20,720 --> 00:27:24,720
I did my time. I'm rehabilitated.

485
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:26,880
What were you there for in the first place?

486
00:27:26,960 --> 00:27:30,400
Oh, if I tell you, it might colour your view of me.

487
00:27:31,080 --> 00:27:33,800
I was young. Nobody got hurt.

488
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:39,840
Well, a few people got killed. A few hundred people. Thousand tops.

489
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:43,040
Two thousand.

490
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:49,200
And it was nasty enough that Stormcage placed a neural restrictor in your brain

491
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:51,240
before releasing you back into the universe.

492
00:27:52,440 --> 00:27:54,200
How can you know that?

493
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:58,840
- Who are you? - Very good scanner, this.

494
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:00,840
I though I detected it the first time we met.

495
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:03,520
And then it started to make sense because we were wondering...

496
00:28:03,600 --> 00:28:06,640
You've obviously got a problem with Rosa, why don't you just kill her?

497
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:09,000
But the answer is, because you can't.

498
00:28:09,520 --> 00:28:14,000
Neural restrictor means you can't kill or injure any living thing.

499
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:17,000
It stops you from doing it, no matter how much you wanna try.

500
00:28:17,520 --> 00:28:21,760
So even if I do this, smash your Vortex Manipulator...

501
00:28:24,680 --> 00:28:27,400
Yeah, there it is. Kicking in.

502
00:28:27,480 --> 00:28:29,400
Tied to your brain chemistry.

503
00:28:32,400 --> 00:28:36,280
You can't harm me. As much as you want to.

504
00:28:36,360 --> 00:28:37,360
Better be nice to me

505
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:39,960
because I'm your best shot of getting out of this time zone now.

506
00:28:41,160 --> 00:28:43,840
Neutered criminal on release.

507
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:46,200
And you come here.

508
00:28:47,720 --> 00:28:50,640
- Why? - I'm allowed a hobby.

509
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:53,080
And yours is Rosa Parks?

510
00:28:53,160 --> 00:28:55,080
This is where things started to go wrong.

511
00:28:56,200 --> 00:28:57,960
And you think you can put them right?

512
00:29:00,120 --> 00:29:02,680
I had a lot of time to think in Stormcage,

513
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:06,320
and I realised tiny actions change the world.

514
00:29:07,840 --> 00:29:09,240
What's your name?

515
00:29:09,320 --> 00:29:12,240
- Krasko. - Don't like it.

516
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:15,000
Listen, Krasko, I will give you one warning.

517
00:29:15,080 --> 00:29:17,720
Go somewhere else. Find a beach, read a book.

518
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:20,200
Because you're a criminal who's lost his kit.

519
00:29:20,280 --> 00:29:21,640
Lost his weapons.

520
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:24,480
You think that makes a difference?

521
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:29,280
History changes when tiny things don't go to plan.

522
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:31,280
You mean tomorrow.

523
00:29:31,360 --> 00:29:33,280
Won't work.

524
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:35,320
Not while I'm here.

525
00:29:37,880 --> 00:29:39,280
Well, let's see.

526
00:29:49,040 --> 00:29:52,960
This is Ryan Sinclair. He's from England.

527
00:29:54,200 --> 00:29:58,960
I'm thinking he might be a new recruit to our youth council.

528
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:03,080
I said he could listen in and serve coffee.

529
00:30:04,320 --> 00:30:07,240
Ah, this is my husband, Parks.

530
00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:11,320
This is Mr Fred Gray.

531
00:30:12,200 --> 00:30:16,920
And this is Dr King from Dexter Avenue Baptist Church.

532
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:18,720
What?

533
00:30:18,800 --> 00:30:21,480
- Martin Luther King? - That's correct.

534
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:25,080
Oh, my days. My nan loves you.

535
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:27,720
Your "nan"?

536
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:30,400
My gran. Grandma.

537
00:30:30,480 --> 00:30:33,240
The elder ladies do love your sermons, Martin.

538
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:37,240
You'll never be short for a grey-haired wife in Alabama.

539
00:30:37,320 --> 00:30:39,000
She attend Dexter Avenue?

540
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,280
No, she died recently.

541
00:30:43,720 --> 00:30:46,080
I'm sorry for your loss, son.

542
00:30:46,200 --> 00:30:47,800
Thank you.

543
00:30:47,880 --> 00:30:49,680
Thank you, Martin Luther King.

544
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,480
She'd be chuffed to know that you said that.

545
00:30:52,560 --> 00:30:54,920
- Ryan. - Excuse me, Dr King.

546
00:30:55,080 --> 00:30:57,000
Yes, Rosa Parks? Whoa.

547
00:30:58,600 --> 00:31:00,480
You want to serve that coffee now?

548
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:01,880
Yeah.

549
00:31:14,600 --> 00:31:17,320
So you say you're a bus driver too back in England?

550
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:20,160
It's a privilege, innit, eh? Getting people where they want to go.

551
00:31:20,240 --> 00:31:21,440
Being part of the community.

552
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:25,600
I'm figurin' you ain't got to deal with keeping coloreds apart from whites.

553
00:31:27,720 --> 00:31:30,920
No. No, we don't do that.

554
00:31:32,080 --> 00:31:33,320
You approve of that, Jim?

555
00:31:33,400 --> 00:31:35,960
Just the way it is. No matter how much they complain.

556
00:31:36,040 --> 00:31:37,480
Ain't going to change.

557
00:31:39,960 --> 00:31:41,880
Back at it tomorrow, I suppose.

558
00:31:41,960 --> 00:31:44,760
No, going fishing at Mill Creek.

559
00:31:46,920 --> 00:31:50,400
You can't be. It's December the first.

560
00:31:50,480 --> 00:31:51,600
What?

561
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:54,760
Well, 'cause it's a Thursday, innit? You know, like a work day.

562
00:31:54,840 --> 00:31:55,840
My rota got changed.

563
00:31:55,920 --> 00:31:58,680
Fella from the depot just came by, gave me the good news.

564
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:07,400
Get what you wanted from tonight?

565
00:32:11,240 --> 00:32:13,000
I didn't know what I wanted.

566
00:32:14,440 --> 00:32:16,280
But, yeah.

567
00:32:16,360 --> 00:32:20,280
Meeting you guys. Listening to you all talk. I can't believe it.

568
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:23,960
It'll get better, you know.

569
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:26,040
Not perfect but...

570
00:32:27,960 --> 00:32:29,640
Better.

571
00:32:31,520 --> 00:32:33,000
I hope so.

572
00:32:34,520 --> 00:32:35,840
It's worth the fight.

573
00:32:38,640 --> 00:32:40,080
Thank you.

574
00:32:41,200 --> 00:32:42,800
From me and my nan.

575
00:32:44,480 --> 00:32:46,480
I haven't done anything.

576
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:54,000
Good night, ma'am.

577
00:32:56,960 --> 00:32:59,080
Managed to get Krasko's weapon off him, at least.

578
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,640
James Blake is taking the day off.

579
00:33:03,720 --> 00:33:05,880
- What? - But he can't.

580
00:33:05,960 --> 00:33:09,160
That's what I said. Strangely, he didn't listen.

581
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:11,560
Your mate is interfering.

582
00:33:11,640 --> 00:33:13,520
He has reassigned Blake's route

583
00:33:13,600 --> 00:33:16,680
to a driver called Elias Griffin Junior.

584
00:33:16,800 --> 00:33:20,000
Tiny actions. That's what Krasko is doing.

585
00:33:20,080 --> 00:33:22,920
See, he's clever. I'll give him that. He knows.

586
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:27,080
He is not planning on killing or destroying or breaking history.

587
00:33:27,160 --> 00:33:31,800
He's planning to nudge it just enough so that it doesn't happen.

588
00:33:31,880 --> 00:33:34,720
Enough of a stick in the spokes to throw everything off the rails

589
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:36,480
and now I'm really mixing my transport metaphors.

590
00:33:36,520 --> 00:33:39,080
Well, he didn't reckon with us keeping it in place.

591
00:33:39,160 --> 00:33:41,600
And how do we do that then?

592
00:33:43,320 --> 00:33:46,280
Now we know what our task is.

593
00:33:46,360 --> 00:33:48,920
Keep history in order.

594
00:33:49,920 --> 00:33:50,960
No changing it.

595
00:33:51,040 --> 00:33:55,120
Just guarding it against someone who wants to disrupt it.

596
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:58,920
Tomorrow we have to make sure Rosa Parks gets on the bus

597
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:02,640
driven byJames Blake and that the bus is full,

598
00:34:02,720 --> 00:34:05,440
so Rosa sits when she is asked to stand for white passengers.

599
00:34:05,520 --> 00:34:06,880
Ryan, don't mess with that.

600
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:08,320
How does it even work?

601
00:34:08,400 --> 00:34:11,920
Charger's here, this setting dials the temporal destination.

602
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:13,480
Pretty simple, pretty deadly.

603
00:34:13,560 --> 00:34:14,880
Now, can we concentrate?

604
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:17,240
How are we supposed to keep history in order

605
00:34:17,320 --> 00:34:19,600
if James Blake is going to go fishing at Mill Creek

606
00:34:19,680 --> 00:34:21,800
and another driver is lined up to do his route?

607
00:34:21,880 --> 00:34:23,600
- I've got an idea. - I've got an idea.

608
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:24,840
Same idea?

609
00:34:24,920 --> 00:34:26,320
- Raffle winner. - Fishing takedown.

610
00:34:30,240 --> 00:34:31,800
Elias Griffin Junior?

611
00:34:31,880 --> 00:34:33,800
- Yeah. - Congratulations.

612
00:34:33,880 --> 00:34:37,920
You are the lucky winner of our Raffle of the Century.

613
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:40,080
I don't remembering entering no raffle.

614
00:34:40,200 --> 00:34:43,120
You have won an all expenses paid trip to Las Vegas.

615
00:34:43,200 --> 00:34:47,760
Front row tickets to see Frank Sinatra and a VIP pass to meet Frank himself.

616
00:34:47,840 --> 00:34:49,600
My wife loves Sinatra.

617
00:34:49,680 --> 00:34:51,480
Amazing, who would have guessed?

618
00:34:51,560 --> 00:34:54,400
Only one condition, you have to leave now.

619
00:34:54,480 --> 00:34:55,600
Right now.

620
00:34:55,680 --> 00:34:59,400
Oh, I can't. My work shift starts in a couple of hours.

621
00:34:59,480 --> 00:35:00,520
We've sorted that too.

622
00:35:00,600 --> 00:35:02,480
Your company has already assigned someone to cover you.

623
00:35:02,520 --> 00:35:05,120
There's a taxi coming to pick you up in 30 minutes

624
00:35:05,200 --> 00:35:06,400
to take you to the airport.

625
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:08,720
- Thirty minutes? - Unless you don't wanna go.

626
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:12,560
Oh, we'll be ready. We'll be ready.

627
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:19,040
Good job Elvis lent Frank that mobile phone against everything I told him.

628
00:35:19,760 --> 00:35:21,400
We need to stick tight to Rosa.

629
00:35:29,760 --> 00:35:31,680
Oi, oi, Jim boy.

630
00:35:31,760 --> 00:35:33,080
How you doing, cockle?

631
00:35:33,160 --> 00:35:34,920
We've been looking for you all along the creek.

632
00:35:34,960 --> 00:35:36,040
Why?

633
00:35:36,120 --> 00:35:37,600
Well, we thought we'd come and join you.

634
00:35:37,640 --> 00:35:40,080
You saying last night how beautiful and peaceful it was. Yeah.

635
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:42,440
This is my grandson, Ryan.

636
00:35:42,520 --> 00:35:43,800
What in the hell?

637
00:35:43,880 --> 00:35:46,320
Yo! Jimmy Blake. What's up, blud?

638
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:49,040
You can't be here!

639
00:35:49,120 --> 00:35:51,560
Hey, how many have you caught? Can I pick them up?

640
00:35:51,640 --> 00:35:52,760
Ever seen a man juggle fish?

641
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:55,200
Stay out of my things. You don't belong here.

642
00:35:55,280 --> 00:35:57,080
And he ain't your grandson.

643
00:35:57,160 --> 00:35:58,880
I reckon we'll be here all day.

644
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:00,240
Yeah, might as well.

645
00:36:00,320 --> 00:36:03,040
Seeing as that bus sit-in is kicking off.

646
00:36:03,120 --> 00:36:04,880
- Mmm. - Gonna be big trouble.

647
00:36:04,960 --> 00:36:06,080
What you say, boy?

648
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:10,160
Oh, we heard that a group of black passengers

649
00:36:10,240 --> 00:36:12,480
were planning a sit-in across all the bus routes tonight.

650
00:36:14,280 --> 00:36:15,520
Not on my bus, they're not.

651
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:17,520
You gonna get out of my seat?

652
00:36:17,600 --> 00:36:18,920
Out of my seat!

653
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:20,240
All right. Settle down.

654
00:36:23,080 --> 00:36:24,800
Have the damn creek.

655
00:36:28,400 --> 00:36:30,000
- He was easy. - Boom.

656
00:36:31,240 --> 00:36:32,400
Don't do that.

657
00:36:35,240 --> 00:36:37,920
Mrs Parks, enormous clothing emergency.

658
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:39,000
Can you help me?

659
00:36:39,080 --> 00:36:41,640
That is one nasty tear.

660
00:36:41,720 --> 00:36:42,960
Yes, it is.

661
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:46,240
And there is nothing us Brits hate more than a clothing emergency.

662
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:48,880
Mrs Parks, I have to go out in that coat this evening,

663
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:50,680
and if it's torn I'll get in such trouble.

664
00:36:50,760 --> 00:36:52,240
Can you take a look at it now?

665
00:36:52,640 --> 00:36:54,280
Double time Christmas bonus.

666
00:36:58,160 --> 00:37:02,880
Well, I don't have any collections until tomorrow afternoon.

667
00:37:03,560 --> 00:37:06,640
I guess I could fit you in.

668
00:37:06,720 --> 00:37:09,240
Now, if you come back just before we close...

669
00:37:09,320 --> 00:37:12,320
I'll wait... while you work. Keep you company.

670
00:37:13,440 --> 00:37:16,240
- I don't need company. - No, but my coat does.

671
00:37:16,760 --> 00:37:17,960
It's very valuable.

672
00:37:18,040 --> 00:37:20,040
I don't usually allow it out of my sight.

673
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:22,880
Thank you. You're gonna make a big difference.

674
00:37:23,560 --> 00:37:26,120
Well, I best set to work then.

675
00:37:26,760 --> 00:37:29,200
Don't let her out of your sight. I'm gonna go meet Graham and Ryan.

676
00:37:29,240 --> 00:37:33,240
Remember, get Rosa out of here by 5:40, at the latest,

677
00:37:33,320 --> 00:37:34,960
to be on that bus on time.

678
00:37:42,240 --> 00:37:44,120
I came back from the creek for this?

679
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:46,280
Four flat tyres and a smashed in windshield.

680
00:37:46,360 --> 00:37:47,840
What the hell happened?

681
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:51,160
- Kids. - Well, I can't drive that.

682
00:37:51,240 --> 00:37:54,160
Oh, no, you go on home. We'll cancel the route.

683
00:37:57,480 --> 00:38:01,320
So much for that protest. This bus ain't going nowhere.

684
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:06,040
Did you hear that?

685
00:38:06,120 --> 00:38:07,840
Everything we do, Krasko's a step ahead.

686
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:10,240
He must have deliberately smashed up that bus himself.

687
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:11,440
We've got to fix this.

688
00:38:11,520 --> 00:38:13,800
James Blake has got to drive Rosa's bus home tonight.

689
00:38:15,800 --> 00:38:17,680
Right, well, he ain't giving up that easily.

690
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:19,080
Well, neither are we.

691
00:38:19,160 --> 00:38:21,920
Ryan, check every bus stop along Blake's route.

692
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:25,080
Tell the waiting passengers that the bus is coming, and they have to wait.

693
00:38:25,160 --> 00:38:26,200
Okay.

694
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:29,200
Do not let any of the passengers walk. The bus has to be full.

695
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:32,280
It has to be crowded enough so that Rosa is expected to move.

696
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:34,280
Right, got it. Good luck.

697
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:37,440
- Now, Graham. - Mmm-hmm?

698
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:39,520
Are you thinking what I'm thinking?

699
00:38:40,640 --> 00:38:43,920
Nicking and hot-wiring a bus from right outside the depot?

700
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:46,520
Hope our friendly cop don't hear about this.

701
00:38:46,600 --> 00:38:47,880
What in the hell?

702
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:51,840
This is your replacement bus service.

703
00:38:51,920 --> 00:38:53,960
- Jim boy. - You again.

704
00:38:54,040 --> 00:38:56,800
I know, I literally get everywhere. Now, come on. Hop in and do your job.

705
00:38:56,840 --> 00:38:58,520
You're already 18 minutes behind schedule.

706
00:38:58,640 --> 00:39:01,560
How do you know that? What's going on? What happened to that damn sit-in?

707
00:39:01,640 --> 00:39:03,080
Nobody else knew anything about it.

708
00:39:03,160 --> 00:39:06,160
Love to explain all that to you but you know us Brits. Very imperious.

709
00:39:06,240 --> 00:39:08,000
Not prone on explaining ourselves to anyone.

710
00:39:08,080 --> 00:39:09,760
So no time to chat. Just get driving.

711
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:11,640
Lot of people need to get on this bus tonight.

712
00:39:12,760 --> 00:39:15,880
Driver James Blake behind the wheel, check.

713
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:43,520
"Bus services suspended." No, no, no. Krasko.

714
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:46,400
Have you always wanted to be a seamstress?

715
00:39:46,480 --> 00:39:49,280
No, I dreamed of being a teacher.

716
00:39:49,360 --> 00:39:53,240
But my grandmother got sick, then my mother,

717
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:56,240
- so that was the end of school. - Sorry about that.

718
00:39:56,320 --> 00:39:59,400
Oh, I got myself educated. Just took longer than I thought.

719
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:01,920
- But you kept going. - Always.

720
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:04,360
An education makes you unstoppable.

721
00:40:05,120 --> 00:40:07,120
Everything here is a fight for you.

722
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:09,840
Don't you get tired? What keeps you going?

723
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:13,200
The promise of tomorrow.

724
00:40:13,280 --> 00:40:16,800
When today isn't working, tomorrow is what you have.

725
00:40:18,120 --> 00:40:20,280
You married? Got a job back home?

726
00:40:20,360 --> 00:40:22,840
Not married and I'm a police officer.

727
00:40:23,720 --> 00:40:26,600
- You're police? - Yup. Just starting.

728
00:40:26,680 --> 00:40:27,800
Not where I want to be.

729
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:31,040
- Mmm. Where is it you want to be? - In charge.

730
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:32,720
Amen to that.

731
00:40:34,840 --> 00:40:36,480
Shouldn't you be finishing up?

732
00:40:36,600 --> 00:40:40,280
If a job is worth doing, it is worth doing well.

733
00:40:40,680 --> 00:40:42,320
But don't you have a bus to get?

734
00:40:42,400 --> 00:40:44,120
I can always walk.

735
00:41:02,840 --> 00:41:06,040
Hey, Doc, I don't want to be an alarmist

736
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:08,360
but this bus seems a bit emptier than last night.

737
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:12,920
No, that's bad.

738
00:41:13,040 --> 00:41:16,480
We need the bus to be full. Come on, Ryan.

739
00:41:16,560 --> 00:41:20,520
Excuse me, excuse me. Old people. The buses are still running.

740
00:41:20,600 --> 00:41:22,160
We're gonna walk.

741
00:41:22,240 --> 00:41:24,920
The buses are coming, I promise. Please, just wait for the bus.

742
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:27,000
You don't tell me what to do, boy.

743
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:30,160
Oh, man. This place.

744
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:33,160
Passengers down, white passengers down. Not good.

745
00:41:40,040 --> 00:41:43,720
- I think that should just about... - Love it.

746
00:41:44,680 --> 00:41:47,480
Let's go. I'll walk you out. Pay you on the way.

747
00:41:55,480 --> 00:41:58,640
Ah, I might have known. Pretty boy blocking the road.

748
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:00,640
Right then. This is on me.

749
00:42:02,360 --> 00:42:05,400
Mate, move your car so the bus gets through. Come on, out of the way.

750
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:07,600
No, you get out of my way.

751
00:42:07,680 --> 00:42:10,920
Just give it up, will you? Cut your losses.

752
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:12,240
You're not gonna win tonight.

753
00:42:12,320 --> 00:42:15,320
I already have. I know what should happen.

754
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:16,720
And even if it gets past here,

755
00:42:16,800 --> 00:42:20,400
that bus is at least three passengers short of what it should be by now.

756
00:42:20,480 --> 00:42:23,960
Parks won't be asked to stand, she won't protest

757
00:42:24,040 --> 00:42:26,240
and your kind won't get above themselves.

758
00:42:26,320 --> 00:42:29,880
- My "kind"? - Yeah, your kind.

759
00:42:30,720 --> 00:42:32,000
Stay in your place.

760
00:42:32,720 --> 00:42:35,040
Mate, you're living in the past.

761
00:42:35,120 --> 00:42:37,400
In fact, you like the past that much,

762
00:42:37,480 --> 00:42:39,920
so why don't you stay there?

763
00:42:41,600 --> 00:42:44,000
Oh! Oh, it worked.

764
00:42:44,120 --> 00:42:46,160
Nice one, Ryan. Thanks, Ryan.

765
00:42:46,800 --> 00:42:49,160
Move the car, clear the route, find the bus,

766
00:42:49,240 --> 00:42:52,760
and Rosa Parks will change the world. Good, here we go.

767
00:42:58,200 --> 00:43:00,280
Yaz! Yeah, yeah, yeah, how's it going?

768
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:02,040
Mrs Parks fixed your coat.

769
00:43:02,120 --> 00:43:06,120
Oh, thank you, Mrs Parks. Beautiful work. Much appreciated.

770
00:43:06,200 --> 00:43:07,880
You're welcome, ma'am.

771
00:43:11,600 --> 00:43:14,800
Don't shut the door. Don't drive off! One more coming on.

772
00:43:18,280 --> 00:43:22,080
Found you. I just got rid of Krasko.

773
00:43:22,160 --> 00:43:24,440
- How? - I borrowed this.

774
00:43:24,520 --> 00:43:27,160
I think I dialled the settings as far back as it can go.

775
00:43:27,280 --> 00:43:28,360
He's gone.

776
00:43:28,440 --> 00:43:30,400
Use the door for coloreds.

777
00:43:33,280 --> 00:43:35,000
Law's a law.

778
00:43:43,040 --> 00:43:44,560
Let's move back.

779
00:43:57,440 --> 00:44:01,080
Hey, Doc, Rosa's on board, Blake's driving, we're good, right?

780
00:44:02,280 --> 00:44:04,120
What's the matter? What are you doing?

781
00:44:04,200 --> 00:44:05,440
Counting seats.

782
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:08,720
Has it worked? Have we done enough?

783
00:44:08,800 --> 00:44:10,120
I don't know.

784
00:44:11,520 --> 00:44:12,960
We get off at the next stop, right?

785
00:44:15,320 --> 00:44:17,440
Empire Theatre, yeah.

786
00:44:20,440 --> 00:44:24,200
Right, come on then. We can go. Job done. History is safe.

787
00:44:28,720 --> 00:44:30,640
- Well, come on then. - Doctor?

788
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:32,400
Don't get off, Graham.

789
00:44:33,200 --> 00:44:36,800
If we get off, there's enough empty seats for white passengers.

790
00:44:36,880 --> 00:44:39,640
Rosa won't be asked to move. We have to stay on.

791
00:44:39,720 --> 00:44:43,160
We were here. We're part of the story.

792
00:44:43,240 --> 00:44:44,840
Part of history.

793
00:44:44,920 --> 00:44:46,760
No, no. I don't want to be part of this.

794
00:44:46,840 --> 00:44:49,800
We have to. I'm sorry. We have to not help her.

795
00:44:54,800 --> 00:44:56,760
I'm gonna need those seats back there.

796
00:45:02,640 --> 00:45:05,600
You all better make it light on yourselves and let me have those seats.

797
00:45:49,800 --> 00:45:51,240
Stand up now.

798
00:45:52,440 --> 00:45:54,560
I don't think I should have to.

799
00:45:55,400 --> 00:45:57,720
Are you going to stand up?

800
00:46:01,400 --> 00:46:02,720
No.

801
00:46:04,520 --> 00:46:07,480
If you don't stand, I'm gonna have you arrested.

802
00:46:11,240 --> 00:46:12,760
You may do that.

803
00:46:21,240 --> 00:46:24,800
♪ You 're broken down and tired

804
00:46:24,880 --> 00:46:29,080
♪ Of living life on a merry-go-round

805
